 uh we have we're a  neutral country by our constitution this  constitution was adopted in 1994 but  neutrality does not exclude and prevent  us from cooperating on security on  foreign policy on defense matters we're  not at the stage of talking about uh  weapon supplies but of course we have  for a long time we've had very good  Cooperative links between our ministry  of defense and our partners between our  police and our partners and those  conversations still continue and there's  projects where our military cooperates  with NATO member states Russia has has  now openly said it was a Russian general  a week or two ago openly talked about an  exit  through to transnistria they are trying  to to join the dots east to west through  southern Ukraine and potentially link up  the Breakaway state of transnistia that  that surely must make you here in  Kissena feel very vulnerable statements  such as the one made by this Russian  general are  are really worrying it's of course  completely unhelpful of course but  creates new tensions  formerly the Russian Federation  dissociated themselves from the  statement so formerly Russia says that  that position has not been an agreed  message at the same time we all know  that the situation is quite volatile  volatile it's risky we constantly see  voices inside Russia calling on on a  kind of bigger and more ambitious War  aims at the same time it's also clear  that the war in Ukraine has not been  going according to plan so here  intentions can be intentions but the  realities on the ground always tend to  safer I think it's fair to say that that  much of the world misjudged Vladimir  Putin and his intentions  from where you're sitting here in this  crucial geographical location what do  you think  he is trying to do and what he might do  next  I mean we're not in a position to  speculate or predict but it's our  responsibility to be preparing for the  full spectrum of scenarios and  contingencies and that's what the  government normally does so if you ask  me what are we preparing for I'll ask  you I'll tell you what we're preparing  for positive scenarios but also for very  negative ones and everything in between  and that's Our obligation irrespective  of what other countries our leaders plan  to do or maybe they don't plan now but  they will plan in a couple of years the  responsibility of a government of a  security sector of a defense of defenses  of the institutions responsible for the  difference of the country is always to  be planning for the full spectrum of  threats and to that end you don't rule  out asking for Weaponry to defend  yourselves if it came to that listen  we're not in a position of now  prejudging of how things will evolve  I'll tell you very frankly that it's  also dangerous to prejudge and speculate  but it we are nonetheless preparing and  we have been preparing for all possible  contingencies  and what exactly we do within this  preparation we prefer not to discuss in  details when it comes to things to to  the Future which we of course cannot  predict
 

